STAGE: solution_design
Solution design: from the problem contract and the data findings, decide the solution plan (one decision per key: task framing, labels, split, metrics, baseline, forbidden features, identifiers, feature direction, cleaning, model families, threshold), each with its reason, the findings it cites and the alternatives set aside. Then lay the decisions on the DCLab workflow WF-01…WF-10 with its gates. Output JSON.

PROBLEM CONTRACT:
{"goal": {"value": "show an honest delivery estimate", "status": "stated", "quote": "We want to show customers an honest delivery estimate."}, "action": {"value": "estimate shown to the customer", "status": "stated", "quote": "The estimate is shown to the customer in the app."}, "unit": {"value": "order", "status": "inferred", "reason": "each row of orders.csv describes one order"}, "task": {"value": "regression", "status": "inferred", "reason": "actual_minutes is a number of minutes, so the answer is a quantity"}, "target_column": {"value": "actual_minutes", "status": "stated", "quote": "The target is actual_minutes, the minutes from order to delivery."}, "label_source": {"value": "column", "status": "inferred", "reason": "the label is a column of orders.csv"}, "label_coverage": {"value": "complete", "status": "inferred", "reason": "the label is a column of the main table, so every row is labelled"}, "prediction_moment": {"value": "when the order is placed, before a courier is assigned", "status": "stated", "quote": "We estimate when the order is placed, before a courier is assigned."}, "time_column": {"value": "order_time", "status": "inferred", "reason": "order_time is the only date or time column in orders.csv"}, "unknown": ["horizon", "entities", "primary_metric"], "constraints": [{"kind": "other", "text": "within five minutes counts as good", "quote": "An estimate within five minutes counts as good."}], "open_questions": [{"field": "entities", "text": "Do rows belong to repeating entities (customers, couriers, machines) that a split must keep together?", "impact": 3}]}

DATA FINDINGS:
- F-keys (info, grain): orders has 3000 rows; 1 column(s) identify rows: order_id. Implication: A column that identifies rows can only memorise: keep it out of the features. Evidence: {"rows": 3000, "unique_columns": ["order_id"], "unique_columns_count": 1}
- F-distribution (warning, target): The target ranges 4.6 to 60 (median 27.1, skew 0.64 below the cap). Implication: A long right tail: compare a log target, and use MAE or a quantile loss rather than squared error, which the tail would dominate. Evidence: {"min": 4.6, "median": 27.1, "max": 60.0, "skew": 0.64, "upper_to_lower_tail": 1.62, "zero_share": 0.0, "negative_share": 0.0}
- F-cap (warning, target): 6.5% of rows sit exactly at the maximum (60): a cap, not a measurement. Implication: The true value above the cap is unknown (censored): squared error chases the cap; evaluate below and at the cap separately, consider predicting 'at the cap' as its own question, and say so in the limits. Evidence: {"cap": 60.0, "share_at_cap": 0.0653, "share_at_next_value": 0.0004}
- F-entity-restaurant_id (info, entities): Rows repeat for restaurant_id: 280 values, a median of 5 rows each. Implication: Group the folds by restaurant_id: with rows of one restaurant_id on both sides a model can recognise it rather than learn the pattern. Evidence: {"column": "restaurant_id", "entities": 280, "rows_per_entity_median": 5.0, "rows_per_entity_max": 130, "later_rows_seen_before": 1.0, "target_from_entity_out_of_fold": 0.2391}
- F-time (info, time): Rows span 45 days by order_time. Implication: Rows have an order in time: validate on later rows than the model trained on (a forward split), and read drift as a reason to retrain. Evidence: {"column": "order_time", "start": "2025-05-05 00:23:29", "end": "2025-06-19 06:11:09", "days": 45, "mean_shift_in_sd": 0.04}
- F-name-delivered_at (info, leakage): delivered_at: its name says it is written after the outcome, but it predicts little. Implication: Ask when delivered_at is written; keep it only if it is known at the prediction moment. Evidence: {"column": "delivered_at"}
- F-leak-courier_rating (warning, leakage): courier_rating: its name says it is written after the outcome, and it predicts the target. Implication: Keep courier_rating out unless it is known at the prediction moment; ask when it is written. Evidence: {"column": "courier_rating", "single_column_power": 0.6274}
- F-leak-recompute-order_time-delivered_at (critical, leakage): The target equals the time between order_time and delivered_at, in minutes. Implication: delivered_at is written when the outcome is known: keep it out. Evidence: {"columns": ["order_time", "delivered_at"], "later": "delivered_at", "unit": "minutes", "columns_count": 2}
- F-missing-weather (warning, missingness): weather is missing in 6.6% of rows, and its absence predicts the target. Implication: Missingness speaks: keep a missing indicator, and check whether the value is missing because of the outcome (then it is leakage) or before it (then it is signal). Evidence: {"column": "weather", "missing_rate": 0.0662, "target_shift_when_missing_in_sd": 0.52}
- F-impossible-distance_km (warning, quality): 12 rows have a negative distance_km, which cannot be. Implication: Treat negative distance_km as missing (or fix the sign if the source is known) inside the pipeline, and ask where the rows come from. Evidence: {"column": "distance_km", "rows": 12}

